write("Economy_and_Money", "Economy and Money", "0 for Books 1-3; 2 for later developments", "1", "12",
 "Books 1, 2, 3, 7, 12 (chapter files); notes for Book 1",
 "../01_World_Primer.md, ../03_Magic_and_Adventuring/Magic_and_Adventuring.md, ../05_Organisations/Magic_Society_and_Associations.md, ../06_Places/Greenstone.md",
 "How money works on Pallimustus: spirit coins in six denominations that also power devices and sustain essence users, farms that make them, and who controls them.",
 "- **Denominations**: \"The basic coin of the realm was the lesser spirit coin. Iron spirit coins were worth a hundred lesser coins, used by bulk traders, adventurers and other members of the wealthy elite. After that, it was ten iron to the bronze, ten bronze to the silver and ten silver to the gold\"; three gold coins are \"worth three hundred thousand units of the basic currency\" (Book 1, Ch61 L65).\n- **Why the lesser coin is the basic unit**: higher-rank coins are \"not very useful for everyday life\"; almost all home and business magic devices, such as lamps and showers, run on lesser coins and higher ranks would burn them out, while larger infrastructure such as the loop line uses more powerful coins (Book 1, Ch80 L40-41).\n- **Sustenance**: ranked people can live on spirit coins; Rufus says a bronze coin will sustain bronze-rank adventurers without stressing their bodies, so it is about not going over your limit (Book 1, Ch15 L92).\n- **Farms**: spirit coins are made on farms using special moulds that cause magic to crystallise, in delicate, very stable ambient magic (Book 1, Ch80 L21).\n- **Why Greenstone**: most areas have too high a magical density to make lesser coins; the smallest denomination they can manage is usually iron or even bronze, and Greenstone was founded because the low-magic desert is a rare place for low-end farms (Book 1, Ch80 L37, L42). A farm that makes a thousand lesser coins would make only ten iron coins or one bronze coin (Book 1, Ch80 L39).\n- **Volume**: spirit coins are Greenstone's largest export, and the desert farms produce almost three percent of the lesser coins used worldwide; most farms are run by the Geller or Mercer families under Magic Society regulation (Book 1, Ch80 L27-28).\n- **Hazards**: volatile weather, a monster spawning nearby or an adventurer using abilities can ruin a batch, so adventurers wait outside the walls (Book 1, Ch80 L43); there is no defence against a monster spawning too close to a farm, and the loss is absorbed (Book 1, Ch80 L44-45).\n- **Control**: \"The Magic Society manages all of that\"; coin production is the main source of its political power and the reason it must stay politically neutral (Book 1, Ch80 L23). An unregistered farm must be found and shut down (Book 1, Ch42 L64).\n- **Escorts**: adventurers escort shipments of coins from a farm back to the city (Book 1, Ch77 L51).\n- **Prices**: armour for an iron-rank adventurer is priced in bronze coins, which is unusual (Book 1, Ch61 L114).\n- **Wealth and rank**: Jason's three gold coins are enough to fund Jory's alchemy facility (Book 1, Ch61 L63-65; Book 3, Ch16 L81).\n- **Permits and trade**: trade and work permits get a person into the Island's trade lanes; aristocrats, adventurers and residents use the wide lane (Book 1, Ch36 L71).\n- **Guild rewards**: during a surge the contribution board adds incentive rewards above the action quota, and guilds earn the biggest rewards (Book 7, Ch12 L15-21).\n- **Indenture as income**: criminals' indentures are sold or auctioned to recoup the cost of their crimes (Book 1, Ch92 L32; see Law_and_Indenture.md).\n- **The Sky Link Company**: in Book 12 Travis Noble's sky link service is sold by negotiated special rate (Book 12, Ch53 L32).",
 "Money in a scene is rank-shaped: lesser coins buy lamps and showers, iron coins buy equipment for the elite, and a gold coin can change a community's fortunes.")

write("Law_and_Indenture", "Law and Indenture", "0 for Books 1-3; 2-3 for Book 12", "1", "12",
 "Books 1, 2, 12 (chapter files)",
 "Economy_and_Money.md, ../06_Places/Cyrion.md, ../05_Organisations/Adventure_Society.md",
 "Greenstone and Estercost have no slaves, only indentured servants, and indenture is both a sentence and a business; in Book 12 the question of indenture drives the System's withdrawal.",
 "- **Indenture, not slavery**: \"We don't have slaves. We have indentured servants. A lot of criminals are sentenced to indenture, then their indenture is sold or auctioned to recoup the cost of their crimes\" (Book 1, Ch92 L32). Jason finds the system disturbing and says both his world and this one need better systems (Book 1, Ch92 L33-34).\n- **Captured enemies**: the Ustei do not accept slavery; \"their culture doesn't allow warriors to be made slaves. For them, capture means release or death\" (Book 1, Ch92 L36).\n- **The Adventure Society agreement**: a clause in the Adventure Society's agreement with Greenstone gives anyone who completes a contract the right of refusal on anyone sentenced to indenture as a result of it (Book 1, Ch105 L66).\n- **Hearings**: indenture hearings are a normal legal event (Book 2, Ch1 L77); the Greenstone court \"was less like an American legal p[roceeding]\" (Book 2, Ch3 L43).\n- **House guards**: in Cyrion, house guards are \"technically a form of indentured servitude\" and the laws \"were very much built to favour the aristocratic families\" (Book 12, Ch48 L53).\n- **Jason's argument**: if a family used \"draconian measures\" to hold people to cheap work, nothing would stop them (Book 1, Ch80 L78).\n- **The System's withdrawal**: the System will withdraw access \"in regions of Pallimustus currently operating with slavery, indentured servitude or similar legal systems\" (Book 12, Ch50 L28). \"The Queen of Estercost and several other interested parties\" ask for arbitration over the withdrawal (Book 12, Ch51 L13).\n- **Slave markets**: Jason's friend scouts Sadi Andali's slave markets, and \"the destruction of the Sadi Andali slave trade\" is put to him as his price (Book 12, Ch52 L35-41).",
 "Indenture is respectable in Estercost and a flashpoint with Earth-raised outsiders; scenes about it carry real moral weight.")

write("Daily_Life", "Daily Life: Calendar, Food, Clothing and Transport", "0", "1", "12",
 "Books 1, 6, 9, 12 (chapter files)",
 "Economy_and_Money.md, ../06_Places/Greenstone.md, ../01_World_Primer.md",
 "The practical texture of life in Greenstone and the delta: the calendar, what people wear and eat, how they travel and what runs on spirit coins.",
 "- **Calendar**: \"twelve months of thirty days, divided up into early, mid and late stages of each season\", five extra days for the solstices, equinoxes and the new year, which falls at the beginning of spring (Book 1, Ch22 L19). The week has six days (Book 6, Ch10 L41).\n- **Clothing**: \"Most people, whether in Greenstone or the delta, wore loose-fitting, breathable clothes because of the heat\"; adventurers on a job wear fitted outfits, often with protective properties (Book 1, Ch59 L30). Elf fashion has flowing, tapered lines (Book 9, Ch46 L5).\n- **Food**: preparation is often familiar, with bread, soup, sandwiches and cake, but the ingredients are mostly different because farms raise different animals and trees bear different fruit (Book 1, Ch45 L8). Jason lists local sauce ingredients with a village cook (Book 1, Ch25 L13).\n- **Housing**: Leust, one of the delta's largest towns, has paved roads and stone buildings \"instead of mud-brick\" (Book 1, Ch93 L5); in the Island, green stone appears everywhere (Book 1, Ch45 L5).\n- **Transport**: heidels are \"the size and shape of a horse, but with scales instead of hair and two heads, each with a horn\" (Book 1, Ch17 L106); the Island's districts are linked by a subterranean, submarine transit line called the loop (Book 1, Ch43 L5), with terminals laid out like train stations (Book 1, Ch37 L83).\n- **Devices**: lamps, showers and most household magic run on lesser spirit coins (Book 1, Ch80 L40).\n- **Communication**: the Magic Society's water link projects a lifelike image of a speaker (Book 1, Ch49 L27); by Book 12 Travis Noble's sky link service carries calls (Book 12, Ch53 L32).",
 "Heat, coin-run conveniences and a loop line make Greenstone feel modern; outside it, heidels, mud-brick and the sun set the pace.")

write("Religion_in_Practice", "Religion in Practice", "0 for Books 1-3; 2 for Book 7 onward", "1", "12",
 "Books 1, 6, 7, 8, 10, 12 (chapter files)",
 "../02_Cosmology_and_Powers/Gods_and_Churches.md, Economy_and_Money.md",
 "How faith appears day to day: crowded temple districts with hierarchies of their own, churches that serve as hospitals and archives, and gods whose rules limit what they can do.",
 "- **Temple districts**: temples line the road to the Island's Divine Square and \"gods had hierarchies of their own\": the Temple of Roads sits behind the larger Temple of Journeys (Book 1, Ch45 L4). The square is crowded with booths selling religious paraphernalia and people proselytising (Book 1, Ch45 L5, L9).\n- **Dominion**: \"Dominion issues the divine right to kings and nobles. It is he who determines who rules, and who serves\" (Book 1, Ch45 L18).\n- **The Healer**: Neil makes a stand in the Temple of the Healer (Book 1, Ch94 L3); the Healer strips the essences from clergy who offend him (notes, Book 1), and ex-clergy stripped of divinely gifted essences suffer a debilitating imbalance in body and soul (Book 4, Ch11 L16).\n- **Knowledge's temple**: in Rimaros it is \"an important resource for the many magical researchers\" with a symbiotic relationship with the Magic Society branch (Book 7, Ch9 L4); the temple is mostly a vast library covering multiple wings (Book 7, Ch9 L9).\n- **Churches and research**: churches, especially Knowledge's, help fund the Magical Research Association (Book 12, Ch1 L58).\n- **Death's paperwork**: the Church of Death issues certification of how many times a person has died (Book 7, Ch17 L104).\n- **Limits on gods**: a god's favour does not mean intervention (Book 8, Ch43 L48-53); a goddess says she cannot see into the astral space where Jason holds prisoners (Book 10, Ch31 L69-75); the gods are \"bound by convoluted limitations\" (Book 8, Ch48 L22-26).\n- **The Hierophant**: the Hierophant of the World-Phoenix is allowed to intervene in Pallimustus a single time (Book 7, Ch60 L27).",
 "Scenes in a temple district should have noise, commerce and rival sects; clergy are practical people with real duties.")
